„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙAI am afraid of you, lest I have bestowed upon you labour in vain.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book/§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses•„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙWBrethren, I beseech you, be as I am; for I am as ye are: ye have not injured me at all.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙWYe know how through infirmity of the flesh I preached the gospel unto you at the first.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ€And my temptation which was in my flesh ye despised not, nor rejected; but received me as an angel of God, even as Christ Jesus.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¤Where is then the blessedness ye spake of? for I bear you record, that, if it had been possible, ye would have plucked out your own eyes, and have given them to me.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ?Am I therefore become your enemy, because I tell you the truth?¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book/§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses”„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ`They zealously affect you, but not well; yea, they would exclude you, that ye might affect them.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙhBut it is good to be zealously affected always in a good thing, and not only when I am present with you.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙSMy little children, of whom I travail in birth again until Christ be formed in you,¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙYI desire to be present with you now, and to change my voice; for I stand in doubt of you.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book/§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses“„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙDTell me, ye that desire to be under the law, do ye not hear the law?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ^For it is written, that Abraham had two sons, the one by a bondmaid, the other by a freewoman.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙaBut he who was of the bondwoman was born after the flesh; but he of the freewoman was by promise.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book/§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses”„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ‡Which things are an allegory: for these are the two covenants; the one from the mount Sinai, which gendereth to bondage, which is Agar.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙuFor this Agar is mount Sinai in Arabia, and answereth to Jerusalem which now is, and is in bondage with her children.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙDBut Jerusalem which is above is free, which is the mother of us all.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines”‚¤text²For it is written,¦layout ‚¤textÙ&Rejoice, thou barren that bearest not;¦layout§quote_1‚¤textÙ.Break forth and cry, thou that travailest not:¦layout§quote_1‚¤textÙHFor the desolate hath many more children than she which hath an husband.¦layout§quote_1ªlast_verse…¤book/§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses”„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ<Now we, brethren, as Isaac was, are the children of promise.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙnBut as then he that was born after the flesh persecuted him that was born after the Spirit, even so it is now.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ˜Nevertheless what saith the scripture? Cast out the bondwoman and her son: for the son of the bondwoman shall not be heir with the son of the freewoman.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙISo then, brethren, we are not children of the bondwoman, but of the free.¦layout ªlast_verse